\section{Scaling, and why it is on}{
Every distance this method measures is a distance across all of \code{feats} at once,
so a feature measured in thousands would decide who is whose neighbour. \code{center}
and \code{scale} are therefore on by default, as they are for \code{\link[=perform_pca]{perform_pca()}}, and the
two functions then see literally the same matrix — which is what lets the two
pictures be attributed to the methods rather than to the preprocessing.

\code{\link[=perform_umap]{perform_umap()}} defaults the other way, since UMAP is more often run on
coordinates that already mean something, such as the components of a PCA.
}

\section{Choosing the margin}{
\code{embedding_scale = "features"} embeds the features instead, and unlike
\code{\link[=perform_pca]{perform_pca()}} this really does transpose: t-SNE embeds the rows it is handed
and has no second answer to read off the same fit. The features are standardised
first and the transpose is then embedded as it stands, which is what makes this
the same margin \code{perform_pca(embedding_scale = "features")} reports on.
Standardising after the transpose would standardise samples, which is what
\code{perform_tsne(t(data))} does and why it is not the same call.

A feature margin needs enough features to be worth drawing. \code{perplexity} is read
off the number of points, so 8 features force a perplexity of 2 and a message
says so; at that size the loadings of a PCA are the whole answer and an embedding
has nothing to add. Sixty features derive a perplexity of 19, and on a simulation
with three planted correlation blocks the embedding then recovers them exactly.
}

\section{What is dropped before anything runs}{
\code{Rtsne} does not accept a missing value, so rows that are not complete and finite
across \code{feats} are dropped before it is called, and \code{design$n_dropped} reports how
many went. A feature that takes a single value cannot be scaled, so with
\code{scale = TRUE} it is left out with a message and named in \code{design$dropped_feats}.
}

\section{Portability}{
Everything but \verb{$fit} is a data.frame, a character vector or a named list, so
dropping that one slot leaves an object that writes out as JSON. In a Python
transcription this is \code{sklearn.manifold.TSNE}.
}

